E[15] = """Document no. 191

1411, 30 November, at Ląd.

Gerhard, abbot of the monastery of Ląd, exchanges with Piotr, schultheiss of Nowe Zagórowo alias Drzewiec, the schultheiss's office in Kownaty for the schultheiss's office in Drzewiec.

In the name of the Lord, amen. Lest things done in time vanish together with the passing of time, it is expedient that every transaction which is to be dealt with hereafter be firmly strengthened by the point of letters and the trustworthy subscription of witnesses. Hence it is that we, Brother Gerhard, abbot in Ląd, of the Cistercian Order, of the diocese of Gniezno, wish all who see or hear this, those to come as well as those of now, to know that, the common consent of our elders having first been had, we have made an exchange, and by the witness of these presents do exchange, by the right, manner and form in which it is the custom to exchange, with the provident man Piotr, schultheiss in Nowe Zagórowo,¹⁾ called alias Drzewiec, and his lawful successors: giving to the said Piotr the schultheiss and his lawful successors a mansus and a half in Kownaty,²⁾ our village, situated there, with the schultheiss's office of that our village of Kownaty, for his other half mansus and all its appurtenances with the schultheiss's office in Drzewiec, our said village, over which the same Piotr the said schultheiss and his lawful successors renounce plainly and wholly, reserving to themselves no right at all in that estate and schultheiss's office, all deceit and fraud excluded; yet on these conditions written below. First, that we have sold and do now sell to the said Piotr the schultheiss a mansus and a half in Kownaty, our said village, for which he has given, paid in full and assigned to us twelve marks of broad groschen, reckoning 48 groschen to each mark, so however that the same schultheiss and his lawful successors shall have and possess three mansi there in Kownaty, our said village, freely and peacefully. Further, from the date of these presents the schultheiss shall have a freedom lasting twenty-four years, and likewise the peasants, reckoning from the time they come, shall know without doubt that they will have twenty years of freedom. We have also given and assigned, and by these presents give and assign, to our said schultheiss one arable iugerum beyond the three said mansi, on which we license him to settle a gardener or gardeners and an innkeeper as he pleases. Item the same schultheiss may build and set up a bakery, a smith, a shoemaker and a butcher's stall on his estate wherever it seems good to him, with our leave, consent and will. The said schultheiss and his descendants are also bound to us to all the services to which our other schultheisses are bound to serve, for which services both he and his descendants shall have and keep one horse worth three marks of broad groschen; and he and his successors shall have the third penny of every matter judged, and enjoy the same freedom as the schultheisses set up in our other villages have been wont to enjoy until now. Moreover the peasants living in our said village shall, after their freedoms have run out, give, assign and pay in full every year on the feast of St Martin a mark of broad groschen from each mansus. Our said peasants are also bound to give us every year two chickens each at the feast of the Assumption of the Blessed Mary and half a threescore of eggs each at the feast of Easter; and at the great court, which is held every year according to the common custom of the land, the schultheiss is bound to pay, give and assign to us six scots, and the peasants half a mark. Likewise the peasants in our said village shall be bound to plough for us from each mansus three times a year, one iugerum each time. And so that all dispute hereafter over the service and freedom of the peasants may be utterly removed, the peasants of our said village are bound to us in the same way to all things to which the peasants of knights are bound. In testimony of all which and for the strength of lasting firmness, we have had the present letter fortified with our seal and that of our convent. Given in our monastery of Ląd, in the year of the Lord 1411, on the day of St Andrew the Apostle. In the presence of the discreet and religious men, the brothers: Feliks, prior; Filip, the old abbot; Jan, subprior; Goblin, bursar; Syfryd, cellarer; and many others worthy of trust, called and asked specially for this in testimony of all and each of the foregoing.

From the Cologne copy-book of the privileges of Ląd, fol. 29, no. 64, and fol. 36, no. 77.

¹⁾Zagorów, once a small town, today a parish village in the district of Słupca.

²⁾Kownaty, a village in the district of Słupca, parish of Wilczyn."""

E[16] = """Document no. 733

Ląd, 13 June 1414

Notarial instrument. Mikołaj Kicki, archdeacon of Gniezno, attests the handing over of the parish church at Zagórów to the Cistercian monastery at Ląd.

Original: Warsaw, AGAD, parchment document 1930. Parchment 290 × 422–409 + 39 mm. Notary's sign: on 3 steps a cross set in a square, its arms ending in lilies that stand out beyond the square. At the crossing of the arms a four-petalled rosette. At the foot of the cross the letter m. On parchment strips 3 seals in yellow wax: 1. round, about 23 mm across, with the Godziemba arms, legend illegible (of Mikołaj Kicki); 2. pointed oval, about 37 mm wide, with the image of a standing figure under a canopy (?), legend illegible, partly crumbled (of Abbot Gerard?); 3. round, about 50 mm across, with the image of the enthroned Madonna, legend illegible (of the monastery of Ląd). On the back notes on the content and shelfmarks (15th–18th century).

ᵃIn the name of the Lord, amen. We, Mikołaj, archdeacon and canon of the holy church of Gniezno¹, make | known by the tenor of these presents to all that, whereas the venerable and religious men, the brothers Lord Gerard, abbotᵇ, and the convent | of the monastery of Ląd of the Cistercian Order of the diocese of Gniezno² had procured that the parish church in Zagórów of the said diocese³ | be incorporated by the Apostolic See into the said monastery, for bearing the burdens of the hospitality of the said monastery, so that whenever that church fell vacant by the resignation or death of its rector for the time being, the lord abbot with his convent might freely take and lawfully keep bodily possession of that church, without asking the leave of the diocesan of the place or of anyone, and turn its fruits, revenues and incomes to the use of the said monastery, and have the church governed by a fit professed brother of that monastery, removable at the willᶜ of the lord abbot and convent whenever it seems good to them, as is set out more fully in the apostolic letters of the late Gregory the Twelfth⁴ and in the process of the executor appointed for this; in consideration therefore of this hospitality and these burdens the discreet man Lord Paweł, rector of the said church in Zagórów⁵, having special love for the said monastery and wishing that the said grace of incorporation should have its due effect, has of his own will and freely, not compelled nor led astray by fraud, given up and ceded the same church and its possession to the same lord abbot and convent, keeping for himself suitable food and clothing for the rest of his life. And the said Lord Gerard, abbot, with his convent, wishing to repay such great love to the same Lord Paweł with a benefit, has graciously taken the same Lord Paweł into the house of the said monastery for his fitting support. For his food he has assigned him the abbot's table, and for his clothing at each of the Ember seasons through the year a mark and a half, and also one tunic of the cloth usually given to the brothers of the convent, of eight ells, and also a fur, if he needs it, of lambskins, and also four pairs of shoes each year for the rest of his life, all to be faithfully supplied to the same Lord Paweł by the same Lord Gerard, abbot, and his successors and the convent; the same lord abbot shall also be bound to keep at his own cost a servant needed for the service of the said Lord Paweł, whose wages the same Lord Paweł will provide. And the same Lord Paweł has assigned and bequeathed to the said monastery, for the remedy of his soul, all and each of the foregoing things left after his death, in whatever goods or sums of money they may be. And we, Gerard, the said abbot of Ląd, with the brothers of our convent, promise in good faith to hold and keep all the foregoing expressed above to the same Lord Paweł, and to keep them we bind ourselves and our successors in good faith; and if, which God forbid, the foregoing should be broken by us or our successors, and the said Lord Paweł not be provided with the said food and clothing according to the needs of a priest's state, as is expressed above, then, lest the same Lord Paweł be seen to beg and suffer want to the shame and disgrace of the honour of the clergy, it shall be lawful for him to have recourse to taking the fruits, revenues and incomes of the same church and maintaining himself from them, in which fruits he is not to be disturbed in any way by us or our successors. And if he should be disturbed, then of our own will and freely we subject ourselves and our successors in this respect to the jurisdiction and coercion of the said Lord Mikołaj, archdeacon, and his successors, and we take upon ourselves the sentences of excommunication and interdict, in so far as he shall have pronounced them against us and the said monastery and the church of the monastery for the foregoing, and promise to obey them and keep the church interdict, renouncing in the foregoing the privileges of exemption of the said monastery and order granted by any Roman pontiffs or other ordinaries, and also the exceptions and appeals and the help of the laws and canons that belong or might in any way belong to us and our said successors, by which the said submission and coercion to be done by the said lord archdeacon might in any way be hindered, which we wish to avail us and our successors in nothing. In faith and testimony of all and each of the foregoing we have ordered these present letters or this present public instrument to be written and published by the notary written below and to be strengthened by the hanging of our seals and those of the said convent. Done and given in the monastery of Ląd in the year of the Lord one thousand four hundred and fourteen, in the seventh indiction, in the fifth year of the pontificate of the most holy father and our lord in Christ, Lord John the Twenty-third, Pope by divine providence, on Wednesday the thirteenth day of the month of June, at about the sixth hour, there being present the religious men, the lords: Jan, prior⁶, Feliks, rector of the parish church in Ląd⁷, Goblin, bursar⁸, Syfryd, cellarer⁹, and Gerard, brothers¹⁰ of the said convent of Ląd, and also the noble and discreet Trojan and Marcin, heirs of Kiki¹¹, and Przybysław, servant of the church of Zagórów¹², witnesses called and asked specially to the foregoing.

Notary's sign. And I, Maciej, son of the late Jan Linko, cleric of Gniezno, public notary by imperial authority¹³, since I was present together with the witnesses named above at the said giving up, promise and ordinance and at all and each of the foregoing while they were being done and made as is set out above, and saw and heard them done so, therefore, writing with my own hand, I have subscribed here and brought this into this public form, signing it with my usual and accustomed sign and name, asked and requested, in faith and testimony of all and each of the foregoing.

ᵃAn initial the height of the whole text of the original.

ᵇabb as in the original, an erasure in the middle.

ᶜreading not quite certain. Mikołaj Kicki, see above, no. 439/8.

²Abbot Gerhard, see above, no. 412/9.

³Zagórów, a town in the district of Konin, the property of the monastery of Ląd.

⁴Gregory XII, Roman pope 1406–1409 (1415), d. 1417. His letter concerning Zagórów not otherwise known.

⁵The parish priest Paweł not otherwise known, though perhaps the same as the one mentioned in the necrology of Ląd (MPH V, p. 498).

⁶The prior Jan not identified.

⁷The parish priest Feliks occurs only here, perhaps the same as the monastery's steward of 1395 (KDW VI, no. 342/5), prior in 1411 (KDW V, no. 191), d. 1418 (MPH V, p. 484).

⁸Gobelin occurs already in 1411 (KDW V, no. 191) and still in 1431 and 1433 (KDW IX); compare also MPH V, pp. 492, 498, and the prior of that name in 1402 and 1404 (KDW V, pp. 27 and 37).

⁹Zewrzyd occurs as cellarer from 1402 (KDW V, pp. 28 f.), perhaps the same as the later abbot Zygfryd 1418–1428, d. 1429 (MPH V, p. 494).

¹⁰Gerard not identified.

¹¹Kiki in the land of Sieradz (Zajączkowscy, Materiały, I, p. 129). Trojan and Marcin were probably kinsmen (brothers?) of Mikołaj Kicki.

¹²Przybysław occurs only here.

¹³Maciej not identified."""

E[17] = """Document no. 755

Gniezno, 16 April 1415

The captain general of Greater Poland attests that Święszek and Olbracht (?), brothers of Gulczewo, sold a plot in the suburb of Gniezno to the Cistercian monastery of Ląd.

Copy: Włocławek, Diocesan Archive, Cistercians – Ląd, MS 3 (Liber privilegiorum monasterii, 18th century), p. 173.

In the name of the Lord, amen. For the perpetual memory of the matter. We, Jan of Szczekociny, castellan of Lublin and captain general of Greater Poland¹, make known by the tenor of these presents to all whom it concerns, present and to come, who will have knowledge of these presents, that, coming before us and other nobles of the same land, Swanseck and Supracht, brothers german of Gulczewo², not compelled, not forced, nor led astray by any error, relying on the counsel of their friends, being sound in mind and body, have duly and reasonably sold for ever to the venerable man Lord Gerhard, abbot, and his convent of the monastery of Ląd³ their whole plot with the square, lying near the church of St Lawrence in the suburb of Gniezno, for sixteen marks of common current money, of Polish reckoning, reckoning forty-eight groschen to each mark, with all its uses, appurtenances and divisions in length and breadth in any way belonging to that plot, and with all right and lordship as they alone held and had it, reserving nothing of right for themselves and theirs, and have resigned it before us in the usual way for ever, to be held, had, used and enjoyed, exchanged, sold and turned to their own pleasure, as shall seem better and more useful to the said Gerhard and his convent of the monastery of Ląd. In witness of these, to which our seal is hung. Done and given at Gniezno on the Tuesday next after Misericordia Domini Sunday, in the year of His incarnation one thousand four hundred and fourteenᵃ, in the presence of the noble men: Piotr of Gunice⁴, Mikołaj of Trąbczyn⁵, Andrzej of the same⁶, Pełka of Kuchary⁷, Jan [ofᵇ] Świniary⁸, Mikołaj of Grabienice⁹ and many other witnesses worthy of trust, called and asked specially to the foregoing.

ᵃSo the copy, but this must be a copyist's error (quarto – quinto), since in 1414 Jan of Szczekociny was not yet captain of Greater Poland. The date 1415 agrees with his itinerary; see the next document.

ᵇmissing in the copy.

¹Jan of Szczekociny, see above, no. 635/6.

²Gulczewo (Golczewo), parish of Marzenin, north of Września (Koz. 1, pp. 54, 89, 412; 3, pp. 560, 671; 4, p. 239). Świętosław (Święszek) occurs 1404–1447 (SHGWlkp.), later for many years burgrave and żupca at Gniezno, Pyzdry and Konin, 1418–1447 (GUrz., p. 171); compare also MPH V, p. 476. His brother Supracht, Czupracht, occurs only here; compare however Czuprachtowe Gulczewo (Koz., l.c.).

³Abbot Gerard, see above, no. 412/9.

⁴Piotr of Gonice, see above, no. 433/1.

⁵⁻⁶Trąbczyn, a parish village south-east of Zagórów (Koz. 5, p. 414). Mikołaj occurs from 1402 (SHGWlkp.), later deputy standard-bearer at Gniezno 1434–1440 (GUrz. C 95). Andrzej occurs 1414 (here) to 1428 (SHGWlkp.).

⁷Kuchary Kościelne, a parish village north-west of Rychwał (Koz. 4, p. 432). Pełka, deputy standard-bearer at Konin 1411–1414, burgrave 1414–1416, then often an assessor of the court at Konin (GUrz. C 472, 363, 412).

⁸Świniary, parish of Kłecko, north-east of Kłecko (Koz. 1, p. 317; 5, p. 379). Jan occurs 1396–1426 (SHGWlkp.).

⁹Grabienice, a parish village north-west of Rychwał in the Konin land (Koz. 4, pp. 260–261). Mikołaj occurs 1413–1428 (SHGWlkp.)."""

E[18] = """Document no. 280

1418, 3 June, at Ląd.

Zygfryd, abbot of the monastery of Ląd, exchanges with the peasant Wawrzyniec Jeleniowicz the farm in Wronczyno Wielkie for the farm in Drzewce.

In the name of the Lord, amen. For the perpetual memory of the matter. Since among the goods of human nature nothing is held worthier than memory, it is fitting that the acts of men be made lasting by the points of letters and the noting of witnesses, lest they vanish with the passing of time. Therefore we, Zygfryd, abbot, and the whole convent of the monastery of Ląd, of the Cistercian Order, make known by the tenor of these presents to all whom it concerns, present and to come, who will have knowledge of these presents, how, wishing to increase the goods of our monastery with the gain of profits, and to bring its fruits and revenues to betterment in the time of our rule, and because (between) us, the convent, on the one part, and the provident Wawrzyniec Jeleniowicz, peasant of Great Wronczyn¹⁾, we have made and agreed such a perpetual and irrevocable exchange: that to the same Wawrzyniec Jeleniowicz and his descendants and lawful successors we have given, granted, inscribed and exchanged for ever our farm in Great Wronczyn, with the fields belonging to it of old and with the meadows there called Obroczna and Dokolowka, for our other farm in Drzewce,²⁾ which he had formerly bought from Brother Henryk of good memory, abbot, our predecessor, for a certain sum of money, namely for fifteen marks of current money, reckoning forty-eight groschen to each mark, by a true exchange duly and reasonably made by us with him, and we give, grant, inscribe and exchange it for ever. To this Wawrzyniec and his descendants we have also assigned and exchanged by these presents, by reason of his beehives, which are called barcz, standing between our estates of Great Wronczyn and Zagórów, which he had and held in our forest, four gardeners there in Great Wronczyn, who formerly belonged to our court. And this Wawrzyniec and his successors shall be bound and obliged to pay us, the convent, and our successors as rent one mark and a half in such money as his other neighbours and our people of the said farm in Great Wronczyn pay on the feast of St Martin: adding also to him and his descendants one weir called yaz, for fishing freely in the stream, otherwise strumyen, flowing and standing between the estates of Skrzynica and Kościół. Moreover the same Wawrzyniec and his descendants are to give the sheaf tithe from all the fields and ploughlands of the said farm to no one else but us and the convent: this being added and specially expressed, that the said Wawrzyniec and his descendants shall be bound and obliged to perform and do any services for us and the convent at our command, and to defend, guard and look after our forest and woods from damages and dangers with our help. In witness of these letters, to which our seal and that of the convent are hung. Done and given in our monastery of Ląd on the Friday after the octave of Corpus Christi, in the year of the Lord 1418. In the presence of these lords: the venerable Donin, protonotary of the Apostolic See, dean of Kraków, vice-chancellor of the Kingdom of Poland, Dobek Kobyła, etc.

From the Cologne copy-book of the privileges of Ląd, fol. 35.

¹⁾Wronczyn, a village in the district of Poznań.

²⁾Drzewce: which of the many villages of this name it was, I cannot judge. In the district of Poznań there is no village of this name."""

E[19] = """Document no. 2000

Trąbczyn, 15 May 1434

Notarial instrument. Wojciech of Trąbczyn, canon of Kruszwica, authorises his brother Jan of Trąbczyn to collect a debt from Jarand, scholastic of Kruszwica.

Copy: Kal. 9175, p. 373. Title: Mandatum ad levandum debitum.

Remarks: 1. On the date see below, note a; 2. See also the next document.

In the name of the Lord, amen. In the year from His birth 1435ᵃ, in the twelfth indiction, in the fourth year of the pontificate of the most holy father in Christ and our lord, Lord Eugenius the Fourth, Pope by divine providence, on Saturday, the eve of Pentecost, at about the hour of terce, in the house of the noble Mścichnaᵇ, wife of Otto, heir in Trąbczyn, the honourable Lord Wojciech, son of Otto of Trąbczyn, canon of Kruszwica¹, being present in person, in all the better manner, way, form of law and style in which he could, made, set up and solemnly appointed as his true, lawful and undoubted proctor, agent, factor and manager of his affairs the noble Jan, his brother german, heir in Trąbczyn², who was present and took the charge of the proxy upon himself of his own will, giving him full and every power to demand, take, collect and dispose of at his pleasure, receive, and make a pact of not asking further, from the venerable man Lord Jarand, scholastic of Kruszwica, canon of the churches of Włocławek and Wieluń³, seventy Hungarian florins, on account of the agreement made by the venerable lords Jan of Niewiesz, dean⁴, Świętosław of Wrząca, archdeacon and official of Włocławek⁵, Maciej Dryja, dean⁶, and Mikołaj of Głęboczek, archdeacon of Poznań⁷, concerning the canonry and prebend of the collegiate church of Wieluń between Wojciech on the one part and Jarand on the other, to give letters of quittance under his name and seal, and in general to do all and each of the things that are needful concerning what has been so received, collected and acquitted. Concerning all which etc.

And I, Jo(hannes)⁸ etc., since at the said appointment and all the other things etc.

ᵃSo the copy, instead of quarto, as follows from the other elements of the dating (indiction, pontificate); but it cannot be ruled out that this is a dating in the Pisan style.

ᵇSo the copy.

¹Trąbczyn, a parish village south-east of Zagórów in the Konin land (Koz. 5, p. 414). Otto, Ota, occurs still in 1426 (Roty V, no. B455). Mścichna, Otto's wife, not otherwise known. Her son Wojciech, canon of Kruszwica, not otherwise known.

²Jan of Trąbczyn, son of Otto, brother of the canon Wojciech and of a younger brother, Tomasz Ocic, hard to tell apart from his namesake who occurs at the same time in Trąbczyn.

³Jarand son of Stanisław, scholastic of Kruszwica and canon of Włocławek already in 1432 (BullPol V, no. 111), most probably the same as Jarand of Niewiesz, later also canon of Gniezno in 1441 and official in 1450–51, d. 1451 (GąsSkOf, pp. 79, 85).

⁴Jan son of Chebda of Niewiesz.

⁵Świętosław son of Alberyk of Wrząca (north-east of Koło), canon of Włocławek 1412, custodian 1426, canon of Płock 1413, of Poznań 1429, archdeacon and official of Włocławek, d. 1471 (Radzimiński II, pp. 147–148).

⁶Maciej Dryja of Modliszewo.

⁷Mikołaj of Głęboczek.

⁸The public notary Jan of Łęczyca (as in the next document) not identified. [Ju]"""

E[20] = """Document no. 1520

Buda, 10 August 1440

King Władysław III charges 300 marks on the villages of Wrąbczynek, Adamierz and Borzykowo in favour of Stanisław Czarny.

Copy: Warsaw, AGAD, Metryka koronna, vol. 109, pp. 11–12. Entered on 27 April 1570 (from the original).

We, Władysław, by the grace of God King of Hungary, Poland, Dalmatia, Croatia, etc., supreme prince of Lithuania and heir of Ruthenia, etc., make known by the tenor of these presents to all that we have received from the noble Stanisław Czarny, our beloved and faithful familiar¹, a horse or charger for one hundred marks of money and of Polish reckoning by a contract of market; and for his services which he has done for our majesty on this Hungarian journey to take up the insignia of our happy coronation, now by God's gift accomplished, we give him two hundred marks; of which three hundred marks of money and of Polish reckoning, reckoning XLXVIIIᵃ gr(oschen) to each mark, together and jointly, assuringᵇ the said Stanisław and his descendants, wishing it, we give, donate, show and inscribe to him by these presents the same three hundred marks in and upon our villages of Wrąbczynek², Adamierz³ and Borzykowo, lying in the district of Pyzdry⁴, to be had, held, used and enjoyed and peacefully possessed by the same Stanisław and his lawful successors with all the uses of the said villages, fruits, rents, revenues, incomes, fields, meadows, plains, woods, heaths, rivers, ponds, fisheries, mills, emoluments, appurtenances and incomes whatsoever [p. 12], for so long until the said three hundred marks are paid to him or his successors in full. When they are paid, the possession of the said villages shall return to us and our successors with full right. Our royal hospitality dues always saved. In faith of which our seal is hung on these presents. Given at Buda on the Wednesday of the feast of St Lawrence the Martyr, in the year of the Lord one thousand four hundred and forty⁵.

On the report of the magnificent Mikołaj of Szarlej, castellan of Wł(ocławek)⁶.

ᵃSo the copy, instead of XLVIII.

ᵇSo the copy, instead of assecurare.

ᶜSo the copy.

¹Stanisław Czarny, see above, no. 1479/1.

²Wrąbczynek, parish of Zagórów; Niedamierz, today Adamierz, parish of Zagórów; and Borzykowo, parish of Pyzdry: royal villages near Pyzdry.

³Wrąbczynek, parish of Zagórów; Niedamierz, today Adamierz, parish of Zagórów; and Borzykowo, parish of Pyzdry: royal villages near Pyzdry.

⁴Wrąbczynek, parish of Zagórów; Niedamierz, today Adamierz, parish of Zagórów; and Borzykowo, parish of Pyzdry: royal villages near Pyzdry.

⁵The date agrees with the king's itinerary.

⁶Mikołaj Szarlejski of Ściborze, castellan of Inowrocław, later palatine of Brześć, one of the commanders at Chojnice in 1454, captain of Bydgoszcz, d. 1457?"""

E[21] = """Document no. 1532

Buda, 22 November 1440

King Władysław III charges 200 marks on the villages of Adamierz, Borzykowo and Wrąbczynek in favour of Stanisław Czarny.

Copy: Warsaw, AGAD, Metryka koronna, vol. 109, pp. 8–9. Entered on 27 April 1570 (from the original).

Mention: MRPS V, no. 4154 (from the copy).

We, Władysław, by the grace of God King of Hungary, Poland, Dalmatia, Croatia, etc., supreme prince of Lithuania and heir of Ruthenia, etc., make known by the tenor of these presents to all whom it concerns that, having regard to the faithful services of the noble Stanisław Czarny of Pietrzyków¹, beloved by us, shown to us faithfully and constantly for a long time and especially in the preparation of the happy coronation of this our Kingdom of Hungary, and to be shown by him still more diligently in future, we give him for their sake two hundred marks of Polish reckoning and money [p. 9], reckoning 48 g(roschen) to each mark, in and upon our villages of Adamierz², Borzykowo³ and Wrąbczynek, of the district of Pyzdry⁴, which we have allowed him to free from the hands of the noble Bartosz Płomikowskiᵃ ⁵, ᵇ⁻ᶜwe give, donate

and inscribe by these presents, we bestow them on him with all the uses, fruits, rents, revenues, incomes, fields, meadows, plains, woods, heaths, all ponds, fisheries, waters, mills, emoluments, beehives, emolumentsᵈ, fowling, appurtenances and incomes whatsoever, to be had, held, used and peacefully possessed for so long until the said two hundred marks are paid to him or his descendants in full by us or our successors. When they are paid, the possession of the said villages shall return to us and our successors with full right. Our royal rights and hospitality dues always saved. In witness of these letters, to which our seal is hung below. Given at Buda on the Tuesday after the feast of St Elizabeth, in the year of the Lord 1440⁶.

The report of the generous Andrzej of Tęczyn⁷.

ᵃSo the copy.

ᵇ⁻ᶜSo the copy (praesens).

ᵈSo the copy (word repeated).

¹See above, no. 1520.

²See above, no. 1520.

³See above, no. 1520.

⁴See above, no. 1520.

⁵Bartosz of Płomykowo (today Płonkowo in Kujawy), not more closely known.

⁶The date agrees with the king's itinerary.

⁷Andrzej Tęczyński, son of Andrzej, castellan of Wojnicz, captain of Rabsztyn 1441, of Chełm 1455, d. 1461."""
